On Nov 9, 2:10 pm, Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:

> More common in my games what I want is blessed gain
> ability over full healing.

I usually prioritize enough gain ability to max the attributes
that I care about, but that usually still leaves some full
healing.  (I don't use potions to max out strength all the
way to 18/**, because that would be a LOT of potions,
and there are numerous other sources for strength --
royal jelly, giant corpses, etc.)

> I figure that once foocubi start arriving I want to
> dance with them to get levels and ...

I usually just kill foocubi.  In the early game they're
more dangerous than they're worth, and later in
the game I typically don't really need the benefits.

> That's because before the Quest I want levels
> to get to the Quest,

I rarely have a problem with that, although it can
depend somewhat on role.

> where after the Quest I want hit points however I can get them.

I *always* want hit points.  After the quest, during
the quest, before the quest, before Sokoban, ...

But a potion of full healing only gives you a small
handful of hit points.  Maxing out certain attributes
can be worth more.  _Which_ attributes are worth
more can depend on role; e.g., Wisdom is only
worth more than hitpoints if it's your casting stat.
Intelligence only matters if you're not a Barbarian.
(Barbarians have such a severe casting penalty
that even picking up books is often not worth it.)
In any given game, I evaluate which attributes I
care about in order to decide how many potions
I want to convert to gain ability.  I alchemize as
many gain ability as I need and keep the rest
of my stack of full healing for hitpoint purposes.

> For other spells, even a valkyrie can use
> Identify at 80% failure rate.

Late-game Valkyries, with a robe and a helm of
brilliance, can potentially cast Identify at 0% fail.
The main limiting factor (in the late game) is
getting your hands on a spellbook of identify.
However, Valks generally can't cast mid-level
spells in combat (especially while wearing
metal armor) or high-level spells at all.  This is
not a big problem, because Valks are too
capable in combat to have any need for attack
spells (between a stack of expert daggers and
dual-wielding Excalibur with a silver saber, they
can kill anything faster than a Wizard can do with
magic missile), and the really useful utility spells
are all level 3 or lower, which is within reach.
Besides starting with a longsword and being
able to dip it for Excalibur, this ability to cast
utility spells in the middle and late game is the
other major factor that makes Valkyrie an easier
role than Barbarian, IMO, despite not getting
arguably the most important resistance of all
(poison) right out of the gate.


